KZ3 patch 1102 (SPU pass 74): the read-mostly SPU dispatch gates in cache lines of their own (PE builds).

Every direct hop, computed-hop guess and lifted function entry reads ~25 runtime flags (spu_hop_ok,
spu_ib_guess_ok, spu_ls_dbg_armed, the fa/fs/fm and drain fast-path switches). They are written once at start or
by opt-in diagnostics, but the linker placed them next to counters other SPU threads write all the time: in K2's
kz3.exe g_spu_stack_reset_n and g_kz3_trace_ctx shared a 64-byte line with g_spu_putllc_count (++ on every PUTLLC
of every SPU) and g_spu_putllc_sync_hit, g_pm_flow_ctx and g_spu_ib_guess one with g_sn_defer/g_sn_defer_ctx,
g_spu_launch_diag one with g_kz3_fma_fb[4] (an atomic add per fma fallback vector). The gate helpers were ~11 % of
the audio synth job's samples (spu_hop_ok 3.8, spu_ib_guess_ok 4.0, spu_ls_dbg_armed 3.0, SPU pass 74 profile),
with the hottest samples on the loads of those lines, and -mcmodel=small (direct loads instead of .refptr) did not
change that.
- spu_context.h: SPU_READ_MOSTLY = section(".data$kz3rm") on PE GCC builds; applied to the header's weak gates
  (g_spu_ls_probe, g_spu_smc_watch, g_wws_code_probe, g_wws_read_probe) and to the definitions in spu_drain.c,
  spu_lockstep.c, spu_channels.c and diag/kz3_wait_acct.c (g_kz3_wtrace).
- spu_channels.c: 64-byte aligned 64-byte fences in .data$kz3rl and .data$kz3rn; ld's PE script sorts .data$* by
  name, so they bound the group and no other variable shares its lines.
Same variables and values; layout only. -DSPU_NO_READ_MOSTLY (all runtime TUs) = the old layout for an A/B.
Upstream candidate (layout of hot read-mostly globals vs. hot counters in the SPU runtime).

Rebased on ps3recomp v0.18.0 (SPU pass 108): READ_MOSTLY on `g_spu_stack_reset_n` and `g_spu_depth_max`.

